Now I can see the issue clearly. The app uses vanilla JS with a `render()` function that completely replaces all `<li>` elements in the todo list whenever any state change occurs. This is the prototypical "DOM detaching" problem.

When the acceptance test does this sequence (from the failure output):

```
get .todo-list li        → finds all todo items
...
get .toggle-all
check                    → clicks toggle-all, which triggers toggleAll() → render()
get @todos               → tries to use cached reference from `as todos`
eq                       → fails because DOM nodes are detached
```

The problem is in the `render()` function: it completely replaces all `li` elements by:
1. Creating a document fragment with new `li` elements
2. Then `while (list.firstChild) { list.removeChild(list.firstChild); }`
3. Then `list.appendChild(fragment);`

This destroys all existing DOM nodes and creates new ones. Any cached references to old DOM nodes held by Cypress become detached.

The fix needs to preserve existing DOM nodes and only update them (or add/remove as needed) instead of rebuilding them from scratch. This is the classic React key-based reconciliation approach.

But wait - the test is a FIXED acceptance test that I cannot change. The issue is in the PRODUCT code (app.js). The fix should be: instead of completely rebuilding the `<li>` elements on every render, the app should reuse existing DOM elements.

Let me think about the best approach:

The core problem is that `render()` destroys and recreates all list items on every call. The fix should update existing DOM nodes in-place when possible, rather than replacing them.

For the "toggle all" scenario:
1. User clicks toggle-all checkbox
2. `toggleAll(completed)` is called
3. `saveTodos()` + `render()` is called
4. `render()` destroys all `<li>` elements and recreates them with new DOM nodes
5. The cached references from step 1 of the test sequence are now orphaned

The fix: Don't destroy and recreate all `<li>` elements on every render. Instead, use a more surgical approach that updates existing elements' states (completed class, checkbox checked state, etc.) without replacing the elements themselves.

Let me implement this. The most robust approach is to use an id-based reconciliation:

1. Find existing `li` elements and match them by todo id (stored as a data attribute)
2. For todos that already have an `li`, update the li's state (class, checkbox, label text, edit input)
3. Remove `li` elements that don't have a corresponding todo
4. Add new `li` elements for todos that don't have corresponding `li` elements

But this is complex. A simpler approach that would also fix the issue:

Instead of replacing children, update the existing elements' properties. We can:
1. Add `id` references to the todo items
2. For each todo, find the corresponding existing `<li>` and update it
3. Only create/destroy elements for added/removed todos

Actually, the simplest fix that preserves element references: instead of `while (list.firstChild) { list.removeChild(list.firstChild); }` and then appending, we can use a smarter reconciliation. Or even simpler: we can just update the properties of existing elements (completed class, checkbox state, etc.) and only add/remove elements as needed.

Let me think about what changes are minimal...

Actually, the simplest approach that works:

1. When rendering, don't completely rebuild the list. Instead, for each todo:
   - Find the existing `<li>` with matching `data-id` attribute
   - If found, update its properties (completed class, toggle checkbox, label text)
   - If not found, create a new `<li>`
   - Remove any remaining `<li>` elements that don't match any todo

This way, existing DOM nodes are preserved/reused and Cypress's cached references remain attached.

Let me implement this.

Here's my plan:

1. Assign each `<li>` a `data-id` attribute matching the todo's `id`
2. In `render()`, instead of destroying and recreating all `<li>` elements, build a map of existing elements by `data-id`, then reconcile

Let me write the new render function:

```javascript
App.prototype.render = function () {
    var list = document.querySelector('.todo-list');
    var main = document.querySelector('.main');
    var footer = document.querySelector('.footer');
    var toggleAll = document.querySelector('.toggle-all');
    var countEl = document.querySelector('.todo-count');
    var clearBtn = document.querySelector('.clear-completed');

    var filtered = this.getFilteredTodos();
    var activeCount = this.todos.filter(function (t) { return !t.completed; }).length;
    var completedCount = this.todos.length - activeCount;

    // Show/hide main and footer
    var hasTodos = this.todos.length > 0;
    main.style.display = hasTodos ? 'block' : 'none';
    footer.style.display = hasTodos ? 'block' : 'none';

    // Build a map of existing li elements by data-id
    var existingEls = {};
    var existingLiNodes = Array.prototype.slice.call(list.children);
    existingLiNodes.forEach(function (li) {
      var id = li.getAttribute('data-id');
      if (id) existingEls[id] = li;
    });

    var usedIds = {};

    // Process each filtered todo - reuse or create
    filtered.forEach(function (todo) {
      var idStr = String(todo.id);
      usedIds[idStr] = true;
      var li = existingEls[idStr];

      if (li) {
        // Update existing element in-place
        if (todo.completed) {
          li.classList.add('completed');
        } else {
          li.classList.remove('completed');
        }

        var checkbox = li.querySelector('.toggle');
        if (checkbox) checkbox.checked = todo.completed;

        var label = li.querySelector('label');
        if (label) label.textContent = todo.title;

        var editInput = li.querySelector('.edit');
        if (editInput) editInput.value = todo.title;
      } else {
        // Create new element
        li = document.createElement('li');
        li.setAttribute('data-id', idStr);
        if (todo.completed) li.classList.add('completed');

        var div = document.createElement('div');
        div.className = 'view';

        var checkbox = document.createElement('input');
        checkbox.className = 'toggle';
        checkbox.type = 'checkbox';
        checkbox.checked = todo.completed;

        var label = document.createElement('label');
        label.textContent = todo.title;

        var destroyBtn = document.createElement('button');
        destroyBtn.className = 'destroy';

        div.appendChild(checkbox);
        div.appendChild(label);
        div.appendChild(destroyBtn);

        var editInput = document.createElement('input');
        editInput.className = 'edit';
        editInput.value = todo.title;

        li.appendChild(div);
        li.appendChild(editInput);
        list.appendChild(li);
      }
    });

    // Remove stale elements
    existingLiNodes.forEach(function (li) {
      var id = li.getAttribute('data-id');
      if (id && !usedIds[id]) {
        list.removeChild(li);
      }
    });

    // Update toggle-all
    toggleAll.checked = activeCount === 0 && this.todos.length > 0;

    // Update count
    var word = activeCount === 1 ? 'item' : 'items';
    countEl.innerHTML = '<strong>' + activeCount + '</strong> ' + word + ' left';

    // Show/hide clear completed
    clearBtn.style.display = completedCount > 0 ? 'block' : 'none';

    // Update filter links
    var filterLinks = document.querySelectorAll('.filters a');
    for (var fi = 0; fi < filterLinks.length; fi++) {
      var link = filterLinks[fi];
      link.classList.remove('selected');
      var href = link.getAttribute('href');
      if ((this.filter === 'all' && href === '#/') ||
          (this.filter === 'active' && href === '#/active') ||
          (this.filter === 'completed' && href === '#/completed')) {
        link.classList.add('selected');
      }
    }
  };
```

Wait, but the event delegation uses `list.querySelectorAll('li')` to find indices. If I use `data-id`, the event handlers for `.toggle` changes need to match by data-id rather than by index. Let me check...

The change event handler:
