      var target = e.target;
      if (target.classList.contains('toggle')) {
        var li = target.closest('li');
        var labs = Array.prototype.slice.call(list.querySelectorAll('li'));
        var idx = labs.indexOf(li);
        var filtered = this.getFilteredTodos();
        if (idx >= 0 && idx < filtered.length) {
          this.toggleTodo(filtered[idx].id);
        }
      }
    }.bind(this));
```

This finds the index of the li in the list, then looks up the filtered todo by index. This works as long as the filtered list order matches the DOM order. With my approach, since I iterate `filtered.forEach` and append new elements in order, the DOM order should match.

Actually wait - existing elements stay in their original position in the DOM (they aren't moved), so they could be in a different order if todos were reordered or if the filtered list order doesn't match. Let me think...

Actually, the `filtered` array is built from `this.todos` filtered but the order is preserved. And todos are just appended in order. So as long as I append new todos in `filtered` order, the DOM should stay in order too.

But wait - I'm not reordering existing elements. So if a todo was 3rd in the list but now due to filtering it's 1st in the filtered list, the existing li is still 3rd in the DOM. But actually, the acceptance test doesn't seem to reorder things, and the standard TodoMVC behavior doesn't reorder items.

Let me also think about the `dblclick`, `focusout`, `keydown`, and `click` handlers that find todos by matching label text. They do:
```javascript
var todo = this.todos.find(function (t) { return t.title === label.textContent; });
```
This is fragile (duplicate titles break it) but it works for the test cases described.

Actually, with `data-id` on the li, I can improve these handlers to use `li.getAttribute('data-id')` and `this.todos.find(t => t.id == ...)`. But that would change behavior beyond just fixing the render issue. Let me keep changes minimal and focused on the render function.

Let me also consider: the "Item" tests (failures 3 and 4) have a slightly different pattern:

```
get .todo-list
contains li buy some cheese
then
as firstTodo
createTodo feed the cat
...
get @firstTodo
find .toggle
```

Here, `firstTodo` is cached as the result of `contains li buy some cheese`. Then `createTodo feed the cat` is called which adds a todo and calls render(). Since render() destroys all li elements, `@firstTodo` becomes detached.

So the fix is the same: don't destroy and recreate li elements, update them in-place.

