Actually, one issue with my approach: when `addTodo` is called, it pushes to `this.todos`, then calls `saveTodos()` and `render()`. During render, my code checks existing elements by data-id. Since the new todo doesn't have a data-id in the DOM yet, it will create a new element. Good.

But what about edge cases where the user clears the input and re-renders? That shouldn't be an issue.

Let me also think about the `toggleAll` event - when the toggle-all checkbox changes, it calls `toggleAll(e.target.checked)`. This sets all todos' completed state, then calls `render()`. My new render function would update existing elements in-place, so they'd get the `completed` class added/removed and the checkbox checked state updated, but the li elements themselves would remain attached to the DOM.

This should fix all 4 failures.

